                                {
                                    "name": "core/paragraph",
                                    "attributes": {
                                        "content": "1889 — La Belle Époque",
                                        "textColor": "gold",
                                        "fontSize": "small",
                                        "fontFamily": "affiche",
                                        "style": { "typography": { "textTransform": "uppercase", "letterSpacing": "0.28em" } }
                                    }
                                },
                                {
                                    "name": "core/heading",
                                    "attributes": {
                                        "level": 2,
                                        "content": "A windmill that never milled a grain",
                                        "fontSize": "xx-large",
                                        "fontFamily": "boulevard"
                                    }
                                },
                                {
                                    "name": "core/paragraph",
                                    "attributes": {
                                        "content": "On 6 October 1889 — the same year the Eiffel Tower rose — showmen Joseph Oller and Charles Zidler opened a cabaret at the foot of Montmartre and crowned it with a bright red windmill. It never milled a single grain: the sails were pure theatre, built to be seen from every corner of the boulevard."
                                    }
                                },
                                {
                                    "name": "core/paragraph",
                                    "attributes": {
                                        "content": "Inside, novelty ruled. The hall glowed with the brand-new electric light, mirrors doubled the crowd, and for the price of one ticket a clerk could share the floor with a countess. Paris nicknamed it <em>Le Premier Palais des Femmes</em> — the first palace of women."
                                    }
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "name": "core/column",
                            "attributes": { "width": "42%" },
                            "innerBlocks": [
